  "content": {
    "id": "p43-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Express $y=\\log_b(x)$ in terms of $y=\\log_a(x)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\displaystyle \\log_b(x)=\\frac{\\log_a(x)}{\\log_a(b)}$, for $x>0$, $a,b>0$ and $a,b\\ne1$.",
      "worked": "For $x>0$ and valid bases $a,b>0$, $a,b\\ne1$, use exponential form and the logarithmic power law:\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=b^y\\\\\\log_a(x)&=\\log_a(b^y)\\\\\\log_a(x)&=y\\log_a(b)\\\\y&=\\frac{\\log_a(x)}{\\log_a(b)}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $b\\ne1$, the denominator is nonzero.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
